{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2011 an article was published in the\nreputable \"Journal of Personality and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5970,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Social Psychology\". It was called \"Feeling the\nFuture: Experimental Evidence for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10559,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anomalous Retroactive Influences on\nCognition and Affect\" or, in other words,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15210,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proof that people can see into the\nfuture. The paper reported on nine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20010,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments. In one, participants were\nshown two curtains on a computer screen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23820,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and asked to predict which one had an\nimage behind it, the other just covered a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27750,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blank wall. Once the participant made\ntheir selection the computer randomly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30990,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positioned an image behind one of the\ncurtains, then the selected curtain was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34380,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulled back to show either the image or\nthe blank wall"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37950,
    "dDurationMs": 4379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the images were randomly selected from\none of three categories: neutral, negative,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42329,
    "dDurationMs": 4441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or erotic. If participants selected the\ncurtain covering the image this was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46770,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "considered a hit. Now with there being\ntwo curtains and the images positions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50789,
    "dDurationMs": 3421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "randomly behind one of them, you would\nexpect the hit rate to be about fifty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54210,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "percent. And that is exactly what the\nresearchers found, at least for negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59850,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutral images"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61230,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "however for erotic images the hit rate\nwas fifty-three percent. Does that mean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65430,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we can see into the future? Is that\nslight deviation significant? Well to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69990,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assess significance scientists usually\nturn to p-values, a statistic that tells"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73770,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you how likely a result, at least this\nextreme, is if the null hypothesis is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77909,
    "dDurationMs": 3181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true. In this case the null hypothesis\nwould just be that people couldn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81090,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually see into the future and the\n53-percent result was due to lucky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84090,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guesses. For this study the p-value was .01 meaning there was just a one-percent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89400,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance of getting a hit rate of\nfifty-three percent or higher from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92220,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple luck. p-values less than .05 are generally considered significant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96630,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and worthy of publication but you might\nwant to use a higher bar before you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100680,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accept that humans can accurately\nperceive the future and, say, invite the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104100,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "study's author on your news program; but\nhey, it's your choice. After all, the .05"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109920,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "threshold was arbitrarily selected by\nRonald Fisher in a book he published in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114149,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1925. But this raises the question: how much\nof the published research literature is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119520,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually false? The intuitive answer\nseems to be five percent. I mean if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123450,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyone is using p less than .05\nas a cut-off for statistical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126630,
    "dDurationMs": 4379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "significance, you would expect five of\nevery hundred results to be false positives"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131009,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that unfortunately grossly\nunderestimates the problem and here's why."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136530,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you're a researcher in a field\nwhere there are a thousand hypotheses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140280,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "currently being investigated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142110,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's assume that ten percent of them\nreflect true relationships and the rest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145170,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are false, but no one of course knows\nwhich are which, that's the whole point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148590,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of doing the research. Now, assuming the\nexperiments are pretty well designed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152220,
    "dDurationMs": 4769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they should correctly identify around\nsay 80 of the hundred true relationships"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156989,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is known as a statistical power of\neighty percent, so 20 results are false"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162209,
    "dDurationMs": 3721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negatives, perhaps the sample size was\ntoo small or the measurements were not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165930,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensitive enough. Now considered that\nfrom those 900 false hypotheses using a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170579,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "p-value of .05, forty-five false\nhypotheses will be incorrectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175139,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "considered true. As for the rest, they\nwill be correctly identified as false"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179609,
    "dDurationMs": 3811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but most journals rarely published no\nresults: they make up just ten to thirty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183420,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "percent of papers depending on the field,\nwhich means that the papers that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187230,
    "dDurationMs": 3569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eventually get published will include 80\ntrue positive results:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190799,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "45 false positive results and maybe 20\ntrue negative results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195120,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nearly a third of published results will be wrong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198180,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even with the system working normally,\nthings get even worse if studies are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202379,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underpowered, and analysis shows they\ntypically are, if there is a higher ratio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206609,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of false-to-true hypotheses being tested\nor if the researchers are biased."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212009,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of this was pointed out in 2005 paper\nentitled \"Why most published research is false\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217680,
    "dDurationMs": 3119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, recently, researchers in a\nnumber of fields have attempted to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220799,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantify the problem by replicating some\nprominent past results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224540,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Reproducibility Project repeated a hundred\npsychology studies but found only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228629,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thirty-six percent had a statistically\nsignificant result the second time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232019,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around and the strength of measured\nrelationships were on average half those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236159,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the original studies. An attempted\nverification of 53 studies considered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239849,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "landmarks in the basic science of cancer\nonly managed to reproduce six even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244680,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "working closely with the original\nstudy's authors these results are even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248489,
    "dDurationMs": 5101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worse than i just calculated the reason\nfor this is nicely illustrated by a 2015"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253590,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "study showing that eating a bar of\nchocolate every day can help you lose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256680,
    "dDurationMs": 3929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weight faster. In this case the\nparticipants were randomly allocated to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260609,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of three treatment groups:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262329,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one went on a low-carb diet, another one on\nthe same low carb diet plus a 1.5 ounce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266820,
    "dDurationMs": 3679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bar of chocolate per day and the\nthird group was the control, instructed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270499,
    "dDurationMs": 3301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just to maintain their regular eating\nhabits at the end of three weeks the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273800,
    "dDurationMs": 3959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "control group had neither lost nor\ngained weight but both low carb groups"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277759,
    "dDurationMs": 2791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had lost an average of five pounds per\nperson"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280550,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the group that a chocolate however lost\nweight ten percent faster than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284300,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-chocolate eaters the finding was statistically\nsignificant with a p-value less than .05"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290420,
    "dDurationMs": 3119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you might expect this news\nspread like wildfire, to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293539,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "front page of Bild, the most widely\ncirculated daily newspaper in Europe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297169,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and into the Daily Star, the Irish Examiner,\nto Huffington Post and even Shape Magazine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302599,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unfortunately the whole thing had been\nfaked, kind of. I mean researchers did"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307879,
    "dDurationMs": 3271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perform the experiment exactly as they\ndescribed, but they intentionally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311150,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "designed it to increase the likelihood\nof false positives: the sample size was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315620,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incredibly small, just five people per\ntreatment group, and for each person 18"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320960,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different measurements were tracked\nincluding: weight, cholesterol, sodium,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324620,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blood protein levels, sleep quality,\nwell-being, and so on; so if weight loss"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329509,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't show a significant difference\nthere were plenty of other factors that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332719,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might have. So the headline could have\nbeen \"chocolate lowers cholesterol\" or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336469,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"increases sleep quality\" or... something."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339319,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is: a p-value is only\nreally valid for a single measure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343140,
    "dDurationMs": 2899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "once you're comparing a whole slew of\nvariables the probability that at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346039,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of them gives you a false positive\ngoes way up, and this is known as \"p-hacking\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351260,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Researchers can make a lot of\ndecisions about their analysis that can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354080,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decrease the p-value, for example let's\nsay you analyze your data and you find"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358189,
    "dDurationMs": 3121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it nearly reaches statistical\nsignificance, so you decide to collect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361310,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a few more data points to be sure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363710,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then if the p-value drops below .05 you\nstop collecting data, confident that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368330,
    "dDurationMs": 3329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these additional data points could only\nhave made the result more significant if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371659,
    "dDurationMs": 4111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there were really a true relationship\nthere, but numerical simulations show"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375770,
    "dDurationMs": 3359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that relationships can cross the\nsignificance threshold by adding more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379129,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data points even though a much larger\nsample would show that there really is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383149,
    "dDurationMs": 3931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no relationship. In fact, there are a\ngreat number of ways to increase the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387080,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likelihood of significant results like:\nhaving two dependent variables, adding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391339,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more observations, controlling for gender,\nor dropping one of three conditions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396280,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combining all three of these strategies\ntogether increases the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399290,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likelihood of a false-positive to over sixty\npercent, and that is using p less than .05"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405220,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now if you think this is\njust a problem for psychology"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407990,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neuroscience or medicine, consider the\npentaquark, an exotic particle made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412760,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up of five quarks, as opposed to the\nregular three for protons or neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416180,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Particle physics employs particularly\nstringent requirements for statistical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420380,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "significance referred to as 5-sigma or\none chance in 3.5 million of getting a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425210,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "false positive, but in 2002 a Japanese\nexperiment found evidence for the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429320,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theta-plus pentaquark, and in the two years\nthat followed 11 other independent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433580,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments then looked for and found\nevidence of that same pentaquark with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437720,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very high levels of statistical\nsignificance. From July 2003 to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442190,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "May 2004 a theoretical paper on pentaquarks\nwas published on average every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446210,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other day, but alas, it was a false\ndiscovery for their experimental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451250,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attempts to confirm that theta-plus\npentaquark using greater statistical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454490,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power failed to find any trace of its\nexistence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457970,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem was those first scientists\nweren't blind to the data, they knew how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461930,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the numbers were generated and what\nanswer they expected to get, and the way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465650,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the data was cut and analyzed, or p-hacked,\nproduced the false finding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470320,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now most scientists aren't p-hacking\nmaliciously, there are legitimate decisions to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474260,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made about how to collect, analyze and\nreport data, and these decisions impact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478520,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the statistical significance of\nresults. For example, 29 different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482330,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "research groups were given the same data\nand asked to determine if dark-skinned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485870,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soccer players are more likely to be\ngiven red cards; using identical data"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490100,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some groups found there was no\nsignificant effect while others"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493220,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "concluded dark-skinned players were\nthree times as likely to receive a red card."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498140,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is that data doesn't speak for\nitself, it must be interpreted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502400,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Looking at those results"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503630,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it seems that dark skinned players are\nmore likely to get red carded but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506660,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certainly not three times as likely;\nconsensus helps in this case but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511100,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for most results only one research group\nprovides the analysis and therein lies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515659,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the problem of incentives: scientists\nhave huge incentives to publish papers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520039,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fact their careers depend on it; as\none scientist Brian Nosek puts it:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524570,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"There is no cost to getting things wrong,\nthe cost is not getting them published\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529520,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Journals are far more likely to publish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531230,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results that reach statistical\nsignificance so if a method of data"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534140,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "analysis results in a p-value less than\n.05 then you're likely to go with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538250,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that method, publication's also more\nlikely if the result is novel and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542120,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unexpected, this encourages researchers\nto investigate more and more unlikely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545990,
    "dDurationMs": 4469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypotheses which further decreases the\nratio of true to spurious relationships"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550459,
    "dDurationMs": 3811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are tested; now what about\nreplication? Isn't science meant to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554270,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "self-correct by having other scientists\nreplicate the findings of an initial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558110,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovery? In theory yes but in practice\nit's more complicated, like take the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562730,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "precognition study from the start of\nthis video: three researchers attempted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566420,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to replicate one of those experiments,\nand what did they find?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569630,
    "dDurationMs": 3329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well, surprise surprise, the hit rate they\nobtained was not significantly different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572959,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from chance. When they tried to publish\ntheir findings in the same journal as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576709,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the original paper they were rejected.\nThe reason? The journal refuses to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581330,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "publish replication studies. So if you're\na scientist the successful strategy is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586010,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clear and don't even attempt replication\nstudies because few journals will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589850,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "publish them, and there is a very good\nchance that your results won't be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593540,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "statistically significant any way in\nwhich case instead of being able to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597410,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convince colleagues of the lack of\nreproducibility of an effect you will be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601070,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accused of just not doing it right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603440,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a far better approach is to test\nnovel and unexpected hypotheses and then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608300,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "p-hack your way to a statistically\nsignificant result. Now I don't want to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613040,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be too cynical about this because over\nthe past 10 years things have started"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616280,
    "dDurationMs": 1559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changing for the better."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617839,
    "dDurationMs": 3631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many scientists acknowledge the problems\ni've outlined and are starting to take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621470,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steps to correct them: there are more\nlarge-scale replication studies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "undertaken in the last 10 years, plus\nthere's a site, Retraction Watch,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628760,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dedicated to publicizing papers that\nhave been withdrawn, there are online"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632540,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repositories for unpublished negative\nresults and there is a move towards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637190,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "submitting hypotheses and methods for\npeer review before conducting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640520,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments with the guarantee that\nresearch will be published regardless of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643970,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results so long as the procedure is\nfollowed. This eliminates publication"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648950,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bias, promotes higher powered studies and\nlessens the incentive for p-hacking."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653630,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The thing I find most striking about the\nreproducibility crisis in science is not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657740,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the prevalence of incorrect information\nin published scientific journals"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after all getting to the truth we know is hard\nand mathematically not everything that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666050,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is published can be correct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668090,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What gets me is the thought that even\ntrying our best to figure out what's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671480,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true, using our most sophisticated and\nrigorous mathematical tools: peer review,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676160,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the standards of practice, we still\nget it wrong so often; so how frequently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680900,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do we delude ourselves when we're not\nusing the scientific method? As flawed as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our science may be, it is far away more\nreliable than any other way of knowing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691700,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697180,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode of veritasium\nwas supported in part by these fine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700190,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people on Patreon and by Audible.com, the\nleading provider of audiobooks online"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705020,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with hundreds of thousands of titles in\nall areas of literature including:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708770,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fiction, nonfiction and periodicals,\nAudible offers a free 30-day trial to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713540,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anyone who watches this channel, just go\nto audible.com/veritasium so they know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "i sent you. A book i'd recommend is\ncalled \"The Invention of Nature\" by Andrea Wolf"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722800,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is a biography of Alexander\nvon Humboldt, an adventurer and naturalist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727160,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who actually inspired Darwin to\nboard the Beagle; you can download that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731330,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "book or any other of your choosing for a\none month free trial at audible.com/veritasium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736040,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so as always i want to thank\nAudible for supporting me and I really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738830,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to thank you for watching."
    } ]
  } ]
}
